$DIR/main.p:12:14: x holds a view of m (m, line 10), which was given a new value at line 11, so x can no longer be used here
    x looks at memory that m refers to, without owning it: the view was made by
    m at line 10. An ordinary interface made from a map or channel in a local
    variable holds the variable's word without a count of its own (section 7.9),
    so it may be used only while the variable keeps that map or channel. At line
    11 m was given a new value, so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use x before line 11, or give m its new value only after the last use of x
    Or make the view again after line 11:
        x = m
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

